ve nine different T-tiles from T2 to T9, forming multiple overlapping chows. With T4\u00d72, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9, and lower end T2, T3, I can form:\n- [T2,T3,T4], [T4,T5,T6], [T5,T6,T7], [T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9]\nThis suggests a very flexible structure ideal for **Full Flush** (24 points) and possibly **Pure Straight** (16 points: 123+456+789 in same suit). Even though I lack T1, I have T2-T9 \u2014 missing only T1 for full range.\n\nBut note: Pure Straight requires exactly [123][456][789] \u2014 I don\u2019t have T1, so cannot complete Pure Straight. However, **Full Flush** (all same suit + honors \u2192 but I have B2 and B5\u00d73, B6) \u2014 wait! I have **Bamboo (T)** and **Dots (B)** \u2014 two suits!\n\nWait: T = Bamboo, B = Dots. So my hand includes both Bamboo (T) and Dots (B) tiles \u2192 **not a Full Flush**.\n\nCorrection: My hand: T2, T3, T4\u00d72, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9, B2, B5\u00d73, B6 \u2192 **mixed suits**, so **cannot achieve Full Flush or Half Flush**. Also, B2 and B6 are isolated except for B5 pung.\n\nTherefore, **best strategy shifts**.\n\nNow consider:\n- **All Pungs** (6 points): Not applicable \u2014 I have mostly chows.\n- **Three Kongs**: No.\n- **All Fives** (16 points): Requires all melds and pair to include a 5. I have T5, B5\u00d73, and could make use of W5 \u2014 but W5 is limited (only 3 unshown), and I don't have it. Melds: I have B5 pung, T5 in sequence, but rest are non-fives. Possible partial fit, but hard to justify.\n- **Mixed Shifted Chows**: Possible? E.g., [T3,T4,T5], [T4,T5,T6], etc., but all in same suit \u2014 that\u2019s **Pure Shifted Chows**, worth 32 points if four such. But I only have three possible max here.\n- Best achievable: Likely **All Pungs** is out; **Seven Pairs**? No \u2014 only B5\u00d73 and maybe others duplicated? No \u2014 most singles.\n\nWait \u2014 reconsider: I have **T4\u00d72**, so a pair of T4. Other singles. So possible pairs: T4, and B5\u00d73 \u2192 can treat as pair + pung.\n\nBut better idea: Focus on **concealed chow-based hand**.\n\nWith T2-T9 almost complete, I can aim for **All Chows** (2 points), **Concealed Hand** (2 points), and possibly **Self Drawn** (1 point).\n\nAlso, **Mixed Triple Chow**? Need three chows with same numbers in different suits \u2014 I only have T and B, and B only has scattered tiles.\n\nAlternatively, **Short Straight** (2 points): Two chows in same suit with successive numbers, e.g., [234] and [567]. I can form multiple such overlaps.\n\nBut more importantly: I have **B5\u00d73** \u2014 a concealed pung of middle number. That gives me:\n- **Pung of Simples** \u2192 no, but **All Fives** requires every group to have a five \u2014 let's check:\n\nTo qualify for **All Fives** (16 points):\n- Four melds and a pair, each containing a 5.\nCurrently, I have:\n- B5\u00d73 \u2192 pung contains 5\n- T5 \u2192 can be in [T4,T5,T6] or [T3,T4,T5], etc.\nBut what about other groups?\nSuppose:\n- [T2,T3,T4] \u2192 no 5 \u2192 invalid\n- [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 no 5 \u2192 invalid\n\nSo unless all groups contain a 5, it fails. Hence, **All Fives** not feasible.\n\nBack to basics.\n\nBest path: Build toward **a standard All Chows hand**, aiming for maximum flexibility.\n\nMy current hand:\n- T2, T3, T4, T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9 \u2192 can split into:\n  - [T2,T3,T4], [T4,T5,T6], [T5,T6,T7] \u2014 overlap\n  - Then [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 clean\n  - Remaining: T5, T6? Wait \u2014 only one each.\n\nBetter grouping:\nOption 1:\n- [T2,T3,T4]\n- [T4,T5,T6]\n- [T7,T8,T9]\n- Left: T5, T6 \u2192 need another chow \u2192 [T5,T6,T7] but T7 used\nConflict.\n\nOption 2:\n- [T3,T4,T5]\n- [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but only one T5\nNo \u2014 T5 only once \u2192 cannot reuse.\n\nWait: I have:\n- T2:1, T3:1, T4:2, T5:1, T6:1, T7:1, T8:1, T9:1\n\nSo usable sets:\n- [T2,T3,T4] \u2192 uses T2,T3,T4(1)\n- [T4,T5,T6] \u2192 uses T4(2),T5,T6\n- [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 uses T7,T8,T9\n\u2192 Leaves: B2, B5\u00d73, B6\n\nThen for B: B2, B5, B5, B5, B6 \u2192 can try [B5,B6,B7] but no B7 \u2192 or [B4,B5,B6] but no B4\n\nOnly possibility: Keep B5 pung, discard B2/B6\n\nBut then pair? No pair yet.\n\nAh \u2014 problem: I have **11 distinct tiles listed**, but hand must be 13 tiles.\n\nList again: Your hand tiles are:  \n[T2:1; T3:1; T4:2; T5:1; T6:1; T7:1; T8:1; T9:1; B2:1; B5:3; B6:1]\n\nCount:\n- T: 1+1+2+1+1+1+1+1 = 9 tiles\n- B: 1 + 3 + 1 = 5 tiles\nTotal: 14 tiles? Impossible.\n\nWait \u2014 mistake.\n\nHand tiles are listed with counts. Total count:\nT2:1 \u2192 1  \nT3:1 \u2192 1  \nT4:2 \u2192 2  \nT5:1 \u2192 1  \nT6:1 \u2192 1  \nT7:1 \u2192 1  \nT8:1 \u2192 1  \nT9:1 \u2192 1 \u2192 subtotal T: 9  \nB2:1 \u2192 1  \nB5:3 \u2192 3  \nB6:1 \u2192 1 \u2192 subtotal B: 5  \nTotal: 14 tiles?\n\nBut hand should be 13 tiles before draw.\n\nUnless... I just drew.\n\nIn mahjong, at your turn, you have 13 tiles, then draw to make 14, then discard.\n\nBut here, the \"valid actions\" are all \"Play X\", meaning discard options.\n\nSo yes \u2014 I must have just drawn a tile, now have 14 tiles, must discard one.\n\nSo hand has 14 tiles: 9 T + 5 B = 14.\n\nYes.\n\nSo after discarding one, back to 13.\n\nNow, goal: form winning hand \u2014 4 melds + 1 pair, or special pattern.\n\nCurrent hand:\n- T: T2, T3, T4\u00d72, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9 (9 tiles)\n- B: B2, B5\u00d73, B6 (5 tiles)\n\nPossible groupings:\n\nTry to find 4 melds + 1 pair.\n\nOption A:\n- Meld1: [T2,T3,T4]\n- Meld2: [T4,T5,T6] \u2192 uses second T4\n- Meld3: [T7,T8,T9]\n- Meld4: B5\u00d73 (pung)\n- Pair: ??? \u2192 leftover: T5, T6, B2, B6 \u2014 no pair\n\nTiles used:\n- T2,T3,T4,T4,T5,T6,T7,T8,T9 \u2192 all T used\n- B5\u00d73 used\nLeft: B2, B6 \u2192 no pair\n\nNot good.\n\nOption B:\n- Meld1: [T3,T4,T5]\n- Meld2: [T4,T5,T6] \u2192 conflict: only one T5\nInvalid.\n\nOption C:\n- Meld1: [T2,T3,T4]\n- Meld2: [T5,T6,T7]\n- Meld3: [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 conflict: only one T7\nNo.\n\nOption D:\n- Meld1: [T2,T3,T4]\n- Meld2: [T6,T7,T8]\n- Meld3: [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 conflict\nNo.\n\nOnly way to avoid overlap conflict:\nUse T4\u00d72 in two different chows, but T5 only one.\n\nSo best chow coverage:\n- [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 uses T2,T3,T4(1)\n- [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 uses T4(2),T5,T6\n- [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 uses T7,T8,T9\n\u2192 All T used in three chows.\n\nLeft: B2, B5\u00d73, B6\n\nNow, B5\u00d73 = pung \u2192 fourth meld.\n\nLeft: B2, B6 \u2192 need a pair \u2192 no pair.\n\nSo unless B2 or B6 forms a pair, impossible.\n\nBut I have only one B2 and one B6.\n\nSo no pair.\n\nThus, current hand cannot win \u2014 must discard one tile to move toward winnable shape.\n\nWhat can I do?\n\nPossibility: Keep B5 pung as core, and build T-chows around it, but accept that I may need to break symmetry.\n\nOr: Consider discarding a B-tile to aim for pairing elsewhere.\n\nBut B5\u00d73 is strong \u2014 worth keeping.\n\nAnother idea: Could B2 and B6 be part of a chow? [B2,B3,B4] or [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]\n\nI have B5\u00d73, B6, B2\n\nIf I keep B5, B6, and hope for B4 or B7 to form [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]\n\nBut B4: only 2 unshown, and previous player played B4 twice \u2014 likely many discarded.\n\nSimilarly, B3: only 2 unshown \u2014 high risk.\n\nAlternatively, discard B2 or B6 to focus on B5 pung and pair in T?\n\nBut T-tiles are all singles except T4\u00d72.\n\nT4\u00d72 can be a pair!\n\nAh! Yes!\n\nSo if I keep T4\u00d72 as **pair**, then I need four melds.\n\nThen:\n- Pair: T4\u00d72\n\nThen remaining T: T2, T3, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9\n\nCan I form three chows from these?\n\nTry:\n- [T2,T3,T4] \u2192 but T4 used in pair \u2192 no\n- [T5,T6,T7]\n- [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 conflict on T7\n\n[T5,T6,T7] and [T8,T9,X] \u2014 no T10\n\n[T2,T3,T5] \u2014 not consecutive\n\nImpossible to make two chows without overlap.\n\nKeep B5 pung as a meld, and use T4\u00d72 as pair, then need three more melds from T and B.\n\nT: T2,T3,T5,T6,T7,T8,T9 (7 tiles)\n\nCan make:\n- [T5,T6,T7]\n- [T7,T8,T9] \u2192 conflict\n\nOr [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 T4 used\n\nNo.\n\nAlternative: Use B5\u00d73 as pung (meld), and look for pair among T.\n\nBut T has no duplicates except T4\u00d72.\n\nSo only possible pairs: T4\u00d72 or B5\u00d72 (using two of the three)\n\nBut losing B5\u00d73 means giving up a pung.\n\nTrade-off.\n\nBut perhaps better to keep B5 pung and discard one of the single T-tiles.\n\nWhich T-tile is least useful?\n\nLook at chow possibilities.\n\nThe central tiles T4, T5, T6, T7 are most versatile.\n\nEdge tiles T2, T3, T8, T9 less so.\n\nT2: can only be in [T2,T3,T4]\nT3: [T2,T3,T4] or [T3,T4,T5]\nT8: [T6,T7,T8] or [T7,T8,T9]\nT9: only [T7,T8,T9]\n\nSo T2 and T9 are least flexible.\n\nSimilarly, B2 and B6 are weak.\n\nBut B5\u00d73 is strong.\n\nSo candidate discards: T2, T9, B2, B6\n\nAmong valid actions: [Play T7; T6; T8; T3; B6; B5; T4; T2; B2; T5; T9]\n\nSo T2, T9, B2, B6 are all playable.\n\nNow, consider safety and future draws.\n\nAlso, other players' actions:\n- Next player has F2 pung, played T5 twice, B8 twice \u2014 seems to have discarded many mid-Bamboo and Dots.\n- Across player has T4,T5,T6 chow, played T1, T2, T7, etc. \u2014 likely clearing low/high.\n- Previous player played B3, B4, B8 \u2014 so B4, B8 common discards.\n\nB6: only one unshown \u2014 very rare \u2192 dangerous to keep hoping for B7 or B4.\n\nSimilarly, T5: only 2 unshown \u2014 somewhat rare.\n\nT6: 2 unshown \u2014 also rare.\n\nSo drawing new T5/T6 unlikely.\n\nWhereas T1, T3, T4 still multiple left.\n\nNow, best chance: Aim for hand like:\n- [T2,T3,T4], [T5,T6,T7], [T8,T9,X] \u2014 no X\nNo.\n\nOr:\n- [T3,T4,T5], [T6,T7,T8], [T9,Y,Z] \u2014 no\n\nOnly viable chow triplets:\n- [T2,T3,T4]\n- [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 but shares T4\n- [T5,T6,T7]\n- [T6,T7,T8]\n- [T7,T8,T9]\n\nSo T7 is in three possible chows, T6 in three, T5 in two, T4 in two, T3 in one, T2 in one, T8 in two, T9 in one.\n\nSo **T2, T3, T8, T9** are in fewer combinations.\n\nT2 only in one chow: [T2,T3,T4]\n\nSimilarly, T9 only in [T7,T8,T9]\n\nSo they are vulnerable.\n\nBut T3 is in two: [T2,T3,T4], [T3,T4,T5]\n\nT8 in two: [T6,T7,T8], [T7,T8,T9]\n\nSo T2 and T9 are weakest.\n\nThus, better to discard T2 or T9.\n\nBut T2 is in hand, T9 is in hand.\n\nNow, also B2 and B6: B2 only useful in [B2,B3,B4], B6 in [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]\n\nBut B5\u00d73 already a pung \u2014 if I keep it, I don't need B6.\n\nSimilarly, B2 not needed.\n\nSo best to discard a B-tile to preserve T-flexibility.\n\nBut if I discard B2 or B6, I keep B5\u00d73 as pung, and work on T-chows.\n\nThen after discard, I\u2019ll have:\n- 9 T-tiles (after discarding one B)\n- B5\u00d73 and one B gone\n\nStill no pair.\n\nUnless I draw a T-duplicate.\n\nBut better: Perhaps **discard B6**, as B6 is less useful than B2? B2 could theoretically pair with something, but no.\n\nB6 is more central, but only one, and B5 pung doesn\u2019t help it.\n\nActually, **discard B2** \u2014 because [B2,B3,B4] is less likely given B3 and B4 frequency in discards.\n\nBut minimal difference.\n\nHowever, **B6 is only one unshown tile left** \u2014 wait: unshown tiles show B8:1, but B6:3 unshown \u2014 ah, my mistake.\n\nUnshown: B6:3 \u2192 three left, so safe.\n\nB8:1 \u2192 only one left.\n\nB3:2, B4:2\n\nSo B6:3 available \u2014 not rare.\n\nSimilarly, T5:2, T6:2 \u2014 okay.\n\nSo no extreme scarcity.\n\nBack to plan.\n\nBest move: **Discard T2**\n\nWhy?\n- T2 is only useful in [T2,T3,T4]\n- If I lose T2, I still have T3,T4,T4,T5,T6,T7,T8,T9\n- Can form:\n  - [T3,T4,T5]\n  - [T6,T7,T8]\n  - [T7,T8,T9] \u2014 conflict\n  Or:\n  - [T4,T5,T6]\n  - [T7,T8,T9]\n  - Left: T3,T4 \u2014 can be [T3,T4,T5] but T5 used\n\nAfter discarding T2:\nHand: T3, T4\u00d72, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9, B2, B5\u00d73, B6\n\nStill 13 tiles? No \u2014 I had 14, discard one \u2192 13.\n\nThen I will draw later.\n\nBut currently, after discard, I set up for possible:\n- If I draw T1: can go for [T1,T2,T3] but no T2\nNo.\n\nDraw T4: then T4\u00d73 \u2192 pung, but lose chow flexibility.\n\nBetter to keep chows.\n\nPerhaps **discard B2**\n\nThen hand: T2,T3,T4\u00d72,T5,T6,T7,T8,T9, B5\u00d73, B6\n\nNow, I can aim:\n- Pair: T4\u00d72\n- Melds:\n  - [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 conflict\nNo.\n\nKeep B5 pung, and use T4\u00d72 as base for chows.\n\nEventually, I might draw a duplicate of T-suit to form pair.\n\nMost frequent unshown: W3,W8,T8,B5,B9 \u2014 T8:3 unshown, so possible.\n\nBut slow.\n\nAnother idea: **The B5 pung is valuable for scoring** \u2014 it gives:\n- Dragon Pung? No \u2014 B5 is not a dragon. Dragons are J1,J2,J3.\n- So B5 pung is just a simple pung of middle tile.\n\nBut if I can get **Two Dragon Pungs** or similar \u2014 no.\n\nIt does give **Pung of Simples** \u2014 but no pattern for that.\n\nLow value.\n\nBut **Three Kongs** \u2014 no.\n\nPerhaps better to break B5\u00d73? No, it's a ready-made meld.\n\nBut compared to the rich T-sequence, the B-melds are weak.\n\nSo best to **discard a B-tile to minimize damage**.\n\nBetween B2 and B6, B6 is slightly more useful (can form [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]), while B2 only [B2,B3,B4].\n\nAnd I have B5\u00d73, so if I keep B6, I have a chance to draw B4 or B7.\n\nWhereas B2 with B5 is far apart.\n\nSo **keep B6, discard B2**.\n\nSimilarly, B5 itself \u2014 never discard a pung unless necessary.\n\nSo **discard B2**.\n\nValid action: Play B2 is available.\n\nAlternatively, discard T9 \u2014 but T9 is part of [T7,T8,T9], which is solid.\n\nT2 is only in one chow, T9 in one chow \u2014 symmetric.\n\nBut T3 is in two, T8 in two.\n\nSo T2 and T9 equally weak.\n\nBut T2 is bamboo, T9 is bamboo.\n\nNo difference.\n\nBut the across player has [T4,T5,T6] chow, and played T7 \u2014 so may be collecting higher T.\n\nNext player played T8 \u2014 so T8 might be safe.\n\nBut not enough.\n\nGiven that, and that B2 is isolated, I choose to **discard B2**.\n\nReason: Preserves the strong T2-T9 sequence and keeps the B5 pung intact. B2 is the least useful tile in hand, contributing to only one potential chow ([B2,B3,B4]) which is unlikely given board state. Discarding it minimizes loss while maintaining multiple 